2026","name":"The Kervaire theorem for groups","status":"current"}],"pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","producer_scope":{"credit":{"kind":"attributed-catalogue-extraction","license":"Apache-2.0","name":"Will Cook, OpenAI Math Atlas; source manuscripts and Lean quotations credited to OpenAI.","pin":"598384f431d532144d03cbabd1c5b384e1610218","producer_pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","producer_repository":"https://github.com/openai/math","sha256":"sha256:2e3a4906ab7653c99a46c607859def831d9e3969ef851f7776724059fc61e4d0","source":"data/families.json"},"state":"producer-declared","value":{"rows":[{"label":"Coefficient-group injectivity for unimodular one-relator quotients","statements":["Kervaire"]}],"scope":"Kervaire's conjecture states that the free product of a nontrivial group with an infinite cyclic group cannot be normally generated by one element. The linked formalization proves the stronger coefficient-injectivity statement used in the paper: for any group $A$ and any relator in $A*\\mathbb Z$ whose exponent sum in the cyclic generator is $1$ or $-1$, the natural map from $A$ into the quotient by that relator is injective. This is the selected unimodular-relator result underlying the conjecture.","title":"Nonsingular systems of equations over arbitrary groups","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/256.md"}},"provenance":{"kind":"computed-navigation","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","repository":"https://github.com/openai/math","rule":"Projection of the pinned ledger; no new mathematical assessment."},"reader_comparison":{"headline_coverage":"agree","method":"Compare reported scope categories; notes remain attributed and are not adjudicated.","points":[{"definitions_to_check":null,"gap":"Howie's theorem on finite nonsingular systems (the headline's lead clause, from the October 5 manuscript) is not stated, not even the general one-equation case (exponent sum != 0).